As a Tech Lead, I know *exactly* what is happening here. Look closely at the error report you pasted:
You uploaded: **CR**
The error report says: **Credit**
This means there is a hidden piece of code in your **File Parsing Logic** (the code that reads the CSV/Excel file before it reaches validation) that is "helpfully" converting your CR into Credit.
Because it expands the word to Credit, by the time it reaches our new strict validation rules, it fails the "CR" or "DR" check!
### The Fix: Stop the Phantom Mapping
You need to go into the Java method where you actually read the Excel/CSV file (usually somewhere inside JournalBulkValidationService, likely a method named parseExcel, parseCsv, or inside your ExcelRowData mapper).
Find the code where you are setting txnType.
**You will likely see old code that looks something like this:**
```java
String rawType = cell.getStringCellValue().trim(); // or csvRecord.get("txntype")
if ("CR".equalsIgnoreCase(rawType) || "Credit".equalsIgnoreCase(rawType)) {
    rowData.txnType = "Credit";  // <--- HERE IS THE BUG!
} else if ("DR".equalsIgnoreCase(rawType) || "Debit".equalsIgnoreCase(rawType)) {
    rowData.txnType = "Debit";   // <--- HERE IS THE BUG!
}

```
**Replace it with this:**
```java
// Just take exactly what is in the file, strip the spaces, and make it uppercase
String rawType = cell.getStringCellValue(); // (Or however you read the cell/CSV column)
if (rawType != null) {
    rowData.txnType = rawType.trim().toUpperCase(); 
}

```
By removing the old "auto-expand" logic and just forcing the raw input to .toUpperCase(), the system will pass the exact "CR" and "DR" directly into the validation engine, and your file will upload perfectly!
